> There's a misunderstanding, my comment meant we're comparing apples with oranges.
> 
> I implemented this operation [in Rust](https://github.com/godot-rust/gdext/blob/107a87e3cb0d6bc7f4685907524682c8d9ce544e/godot-ffi/src/godot_ffi.rs#L218-L220), without `array_ref` **and** without full destroy + create. It uses pointer-swap. Now I'm not sure if the same is possible in C++ since `operator=` is a bit more involved. But it should be explored.

Yes, it's possible in the rvalue assignment operator. We do this a few times in the engine as well (though I forget where exactly).

```cpp
void operator=(Other &&p_other) {
    SWAP(ptr, p_other.ptr);
}
```

Though notably in C++ it would be more common to see the `void operator=(const Other &p_other)` form, since C++ doesn't default to move semantics.

Imagine this (C++ pseudocode):
```cpp
Array* out_slot; // godot-provided, default-initialized
Array result;    // our return value


// --------- Your approach ---------
*out_slot = result; 

// which might do something like:
out_slot->~Array();           // destroy in-place.
new (out_slot) Array(result); // copy-construct -> inc_ref.
// out_slot now stores our result.
// RAII will destroy (populated) result -> dec_ref.


// --------- My approach ---------
swap(*out_slot, result);
// out_slot now stores our result.
// RAII will destroy (now empty) result -> cheap.
```

@Bromeon  sorry for being stubborn on this.
You are correct. In isolation the return itself does not need to increment the refcounted value before the move; provided that in the usercode the value was cloned or ref counted or is a new value.
In this case, with this workflow, it would be impossible to take advantage of array_ref in your macros.

In the case of godot-cpp if I am reading this correctly their [PtrToArg](https://github.com/godotengine/godot-cpp/blob/master/include/godot_cpp/core/method_ptrcall.hpp#L62) functions will be invoking the operator= overload. In their case updating the operator= would be a benefit. These are used for the returns in [call_with_ptr_args_ret_helper](https://github.com/godotengine/godot-cpp/blob/87c6629ffe0c89af48bf4df866a3772b53da7a05/include/godot_cpp/core/binder_common.hpp#L202C6-L202C35)

@Ivorforce is that correct?

The destructor+constructor as pseudocode is (assuming no operator overloads or raii auto-destructors were implemented)
```
//current godot-cpp operator=
delete(dest)
dest= ref(source) //constructor1 iirc does not invoke any new, doesn't copy, it just does a ref count.

//array_ref
delete_and_ref(dest, source)

//Rust return implementation
//usercode
user_func(self) -> Array {
  return self.clone(Array)
}
ptrtoarg(self, ret){
  swap(ret, self)
  delete(self)
}
```
